+<body>
+  <header class="masthead">
+    <div class="masthead-inner">
+      <div>
+        <p class="eyebrow">Product manager's field guide</p>
+        <h1>Understanding the Fire Inspection Tool Tech Stack</h1>
+        <p class="dek">Why the architecture exists, what the kickoff prompt really does, what is reusable, and how the same approach applies to dealer backend and panel firmware products.</p>
+      </div>
+      <aside class="thesis" aria-label="Central idea">
+        <strong>The central idea</strong>
+        The stack is the set of building materials. The kickoff prompt is the construction process, quality plan, and operating manual.
+      </aside>
+    </div>
+  </header>
+
+  <nav aria-label="Page sections">
+    <div class="nav-inner">
+      <a href="#distinction">Stack vs. prompt</a>
+      <a href="#product">The product</a>
+      <a href="#layers">Technology layers</a>
+      <a href="#origin">How it evolved</a>
+      <a href="#without">Without the prompt</a>
+      <a href="#minimum">Reusable minimum</a>
+      <a href="#fit">Project fit</a>
+      <a href="#examples">Your product world</a>
+      <a href="#improvements">Improvements</a>
+    </div>
+  </nav>
+
+  <main>
+    <section id="distinction">
+      <span class="section-number">01 / The distinction</span>
+      <h2>Two documents, two different jobs</h2>
+      <p class="lead">The Word document names the technologies and broad architecture. The kickoff prompt converts those choices into repeatable instructions that an AI agent can execute and verify.</p>
+      <div class="two-column">
+        <article class="comparison">
+          <span class="label">The tech stack</span>
+          <h3>What will be used</h3>
+          <p>React, TypeScript, C#, SQL Server, tests, automation, and separate frontend and backend repositories. It is a blueprint of the major building materials.</p>
+        </article>
+        <article class="comparison">
+          <span class="label">The kickoff prompt</span>
+          <h3>How it will be built</h3>
+          <p>Installation, architecture, folder boundaries, conventions, verification gates, documentation, and handoff. It is the repeatable construction and inspection process.</p>
+        </article>
+      </div>
+      <div class="callout">
+        <h3>Why the distinction matters</h3>
+        <p>“Use React and C#” leaves hundreds of decisions unresolved. The prompt removes ambiguity about where code belongs, how services communicate, what counts as complete, and how future AI sessions should continue the work.</p>
+      </div>
+    </section>
+
+    <section id="product">
+      <span class="section-number">02 / The actual product</span>
+      <h2>This is an operational system, not merely a website</h2>
+      <p class="lead">The application must collect evidence, enforce rules, preserve history, and produce formal output. That is a much larger responsibility than displaying information on web pages.</p>
+      <div class="flow" aria-label="Inspection workflow">
+        <div class="flow-step"><span>01</span>Import or manually enter equipment</div>
+        <div class="flow-step"><span>02</span>Create an inspection and test each device</div>
+        <div class="flow-step"><span>03</span>Record pass, fail, reasons, and corrections</div>
+        <div class="flow-step"><span>04</span>Finalize and generate the inspection report</div>
+      </div>
+      <p>The full system also needs authentication, dealer and account permissions, durable records, validation, audit history, report storage, deployment, and recovery from errors. That is why a full-stack architecture was selected instead of a spreadsheet, static page, or quick prototype.</p>
+      <div class="callout danger">
+        <h3>Important: technology does not create regulatory compliance</h3>
+        <p>A well-tested PDF is not automatically a compliant inspection report. Compliance depends on the applicable jurisdiction, Authority Having Jurisdiction, NFPA standard and edition, local amendments, required fields, testing frequency, licensing, signatures, retention, and correction rules. Those must be captured as product requirements and reviewed by qualified domain experts.</p>
+      </div>
+    </section>
+
+    <section id="layers">
+      <span class="section-number">03 / Technology layers</span>
+      <h2>Why every part is there</h2>
+      <p class="lead">The choices are optimized for a business application and for AI-assisted development. Each tool either gives structure to the product or gives rapid feedback when implementation is wrong.</p>
+      <div class="table-wrap">
+        <table>
+          <thead>
+            <tr><th>Technology or pattern</th><th>Job in the system</th><th>Why it helps</th></tr>
+          </thead>
+          <tbody>
+            <tr><td><strong>React</strong></td><td>Builds the dealer-facing interface</td><td>Well suited to forms, equipment tables, filters, inspection workflows, and reusable controls.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td><strong>TypeScript</strong></td><td>Adds type checking to frontend code</td><td>Detects incorrect data and API usage before the application runs, giving both developers and AI precise feedback.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td><strong>React Router</strong></td><td>Organizes pages and navigation</td><td>Provides predictable routes for sites, systems, inspections, equipment, and reports.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td><strong>Tailwind + shadcn/ui</strong></td><td>Supplies styling and controls</td><td>Creates consistent forms, dialogs, tables, buttons, and error states without inventing every component.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td><strong>TanStack Query</strong></td><td>Manages server data in the browser</td><td>Standardizes loading, caching, refreshes, errors, and updates after records change.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td><strong>Zod</strong></td><td>Validates browser form data</td><td>Stops incomplete or malformed inspection data before it is submitted.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td><strong>react-intl</strong></td><td>Centralizes visible language</td><td>Prepares for localization and prevents text from being scattered through components.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td><strong>C# + ASP.NET Core</strong></td><td>Implements APIs and business rules</td><td>Strong typing, mature enterprise tooling, and a close fit with the internal company environment.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td><strong>EF Core</strong></td><td>Maps C# models to database data</td><td>Allows services to query and update records through a consistent data model.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td><strong>SQL Server</strong></td><td>Stores durable records</td><td>Fits relational inspection data, constraints, transactions, auditability, and reporting.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td><strong>DbUp SQL scripts</strong></td><td>Controls database structure</td><td>Produces a reviewable, deterministic history in which SQL is the schema source of truth.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td><strong>.NET Aspire</strong></td><td>Starts local backend resources</td><td>Launches the database, migrations, and API in dependency order with one command.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td><strong>OIDC + JWT</strong></td><td>Authenticates users and API requests</td><td>Supports identity systems such as Keycloak, Entra ID, or Auth0 without coupling the whole app to one provider.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td><strong>MSTest + Vitest</strong></td><td>Tests isolated behaviors</td><td>Catches business-rule and component regressions quickly.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td><strong>Playwright + Axe</strong></td><td>Tests browser workflows and accessibility</td><td>Proves that realistic dealer actions work and catches common accessibility failures.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td><strong>Biome</strong></td><td>Checks and formats frontend code</td><td>Provides a single, consistent source of code-quality feedback.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td><strong>GitHub Actions</strong></td><td>Runs checks on proposed changes</td><td>Prevents unbuildable, untested, or insecure changes from silently reaching the main branch.</td></tr>
+          </tbody>
+        </table>
+      </div>
+    </section>
+
+    <section id="origin">
+      <span class="section-number">04 / How it likely evolved</span>
+      <h2>The prompt reads like accumulated experience</h2>
+      <p class="lead">Its language suggests your coworker worked backward from problems encountered while creating a real AI-assisted production application.</p>
+      <ol class="timeline">
+        <li><div><strong>AI behavior drifted between sessions.</strong> Repository-level Copilot instructions were added to preserve decisions after chat context disappeared.</div></li>
+        <li><div><strong>Setup failed on missing tools.</strong> Phase 0A was added to detect dependencies, explain them, request approval, install them, and verify them.</div></li>
+        <li><div><strong>Vague requirements caused invention.</strong> Phase 0B forces an interview about users, entities, relationships, and the first workflow.</div></li>
+        <li><div><strong>Frontend and backend work became disconnected.</strong> The prompt requires one vertical slice that crosses database, API, interface, and tests.</div></li>
+        <li><div><strong>Local database setup was fragile.</strong> Aspire became the one-command path for database, migrations, and API startup.</div></li>
+        <li><div><strong>Database models risked drifting.</strong> SQL scripts were declared the sole schema authority, with EF entities written to match.</div></li>
+        <li><div><strong>UI implementation became inconsistent.</strong> Styling, breakpoints, visible text, and network calls were each constrained to predictable patterns.</div></li>
+        <li><div><strong>Generated files looked finished without working.</strong> Every phase received a build, test, or live behavior gate.</div></li>
+      </ol>
+      <p>The result is best understood as a collection of guardrails based on observed failure modes, not simply a fashionable list of libraries.</p>
+    </section>
+
+    <section id="without">
+      <span class="section-number">05 / Removing the prompt</span>
+      <h2>The stack would still work, but the outcome would be less predictable</h2>
+      <p>A developer can use React, C#, and SQL Server without this prompt. The prompt is not a technical prerequisite. It is a consistency and quality mechanism.</p>
+      <ul class="failure-list">
+        <li>Frontend and backend might be placed in a different repository structure.</li>
+        <li>Minimal APIs might replace the requested controller structure.</li>
+        <li>EF migrations might replace the SQL source of truth.</li>
+        <li>Database entities might be exposed directly through the API.</li>
+        <li>Network calls might be scattered through UI components.</li>
+        <li>Authentication might be postponed until late development.</li>
+        <li>Visible English text might be hard-coded everywhere.</li>
+        <li>Responsive behavior and mobile tests might be skipped.</li>
+        <li>The database might be mocked instead of testing real persistence.</li>
+        <li>Generated code might never be compiled or started.</li>
+        <li>Later AI sessions might introduce conflicting patterns.</li>
+        <li>Missing installations might be left for you to diagnose.</li>
+      </ul>
+      <div class="callout">
+        <h3>Not every alternative is wrong</h3>
