</head>
<body>
  <header>
    <div class="header-inner">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Product definition</p>
        <h1>Connected Envelope Budget</h1>
        <p class="dek">A bank-connected budgeting product that turns new credit-card activity into an interactive text conversation, helping users consciously place every purchase into a persistent envelope.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="version-box">
        <strong>Discovery status</strong>
        Three interview rounds completed. Revised after bank-connection, guided-onboarding, merchant-cleanup, and deferred-spending review.
      </aside>
    </div>
  </header>

  <nav aria-label="Document sections">
    <div class="nav-inner">
      <a href="#intent">Intent</a>
      <a href="#workflow">Workflow</a>
      <a href="#onboarding">Onboarding</a>
      <a href="#budget">Budget model</a>
      <a href="#deferred">Spread spending</a>
      <a href="#transactions">Transactions</a>
      <a href="#sms">SMS</a>
      <a href="#mvp">MVP</a>
      <a href="#decisions">Open decisions</a>
      <a href="#acceptance">Acceptance</a>
    </div>
  </nav>

  <main>
    <section id="intent">
      <span class="section-number">01 / Intent</span>
      <h2>Make every purchase visible and intentional</h2>
      <p class="lead">The product replaces manual spreadsheet entry with a guided envelope-budgeting experience. It connects to credit-card accounts, detects recent charges, cleans up bank descriptions, and asks the user by text where each charge belongs.</p>
      <div class="grid-2">
        <article class="panel">
          <h3>Problem today</h3>
          <p>Bank alerts provide awareness, but they do not connect purchases to a budget. The user must read each alert and manually re-enter it into spreadsheet categories.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="panel">
          <h3>Desired outcome</h3>
          <p>The app becomes the interactive notification layer: it reports the purchase, proposes a category, accepts a reply, updates the envelope, and warns when spending approaches or exceeds the plan.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="callout info">
        <h3>Product principle</h3>
        <p>The application may assist with merchant cleanup and category suggestions, but the user remains physically involved in categorizing purchases. It should create a small moment of spending awareness rather than making budgeting invisible.</p>
      </div>
      <p><strong>Initial audience:</strong> designed from a single-user use case, but account creation, templates, bank connections, and configurable categories make the concept suitable for additional individual users later.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="workflow">
      <span class="section-number">02 / Primary workflow</span>
      <h2>The app sends the charge alert</h2>
      <div class="flow" aria-label="Connected transaction workflow">
        <div class="step"><span class="step-number">01</span>User securely connects a supported credit-card account.</div>
        <div class="step"><span class="step-number">02</span>Bank-data provider reports a new charge to the application.</div>
        <div class="step"><span class="step-number">03</span>App cleans the merchant name and suggests a category by text.</div>
        <div class="step"><span class="step-number">04</span>User confirms or chooses another category; envelope updates.</div>
      </div>
      <p>The first bank-connected version supports credit cards only. The app notifies as soon as the provider reports a transaction. The user accepts that these notifications may arrive later than native bank alerts because aggregator timing differs by issuer.</p>
      <div class="callout">
        <h3>Timing reality</h3>
        <p>A connected-bank provider does not guarantee real-time delivery. “As soon as available” may still mean minutes, hours, or occasionally longer after purchase. This is a product constraint that must be communicated honestly rather than presented as identical to native bank alerts.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="onboarding">
      <span class="section-number">03 / Guided onboarding</span>
      <h2>Start with a useful structure, not a blank screen</h2>
      <p class="lead">Account setup should reduce decision fatigue while keeping every recommendation editable.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Create credentials using username and password.</li>
        <li>Enter the monthly take-home amount available for budgeting.</li>
        <li>Choose a category template that creates starter envelopes.</li>
        <li>Choose an allocation model, beginning with standard 50/30/20.</li>
        <li>Review suggested category dollar amounts calculated from the budget.</li>
        <li>Rename, add, archive, and adjust envelopes before confirming setup.</li>
        <li>Connect one or more supported credit-card accounts.</li>
        <li>Confirm the mobile number used for interactive notifications.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="grid-2">
        <article class="panel">
          <span class="status">Category template</span>
          <h3>Defines what the user budgets</h3>
          <p>Creates starter categories such as housing, groceries, dining, transportation, insurance, personal spending, emergency savings, and long-term savings.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="panel">
          <span class="status">Allocation model</span>
          <h3>Defines how the budget is divided</h3>
          <p>50% Needs, 30% Wants, and 20% Savings is the initial model. Users may customize the percentages and then adjust every suggested dollar amount.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p>Templates provide starter categories and suggested amounts. They do not lock the user into fixed names or allocations. The template and allocation model are separate choices: first choose the category structure, then choose how the monthly total should be divided.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="budget">
      <span class="section-number">04 / Envelope model</span>
      <h2>Balances persist across months</h2>
      <p>Each active category has a monthly contribution. The user explicitly closes the prior month before the next contribution is added. Both surplus and overspending roll forward.</p>
      <div class="formula"><strong>Next envelope balance</strong><br>prior balance + monthly contribution - ordinary spending - scheduled spread installments</div>
      <p>Category targets may total more than the monthly budgeting amount. The interface must visibly warn about over-allocation but must not prevent the user from saving it.</p>
      <div class="grid-2">
        <article class="panel">
          <h3>Category lifecycle</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Create and rename categories.</li>
            <li>Change monthly contributions.</li>
            <li>Set near-limit warning thresholds.</li>
            <li>Archive categories while preserving history.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
        <article class="panel">
          <h3>Historical corrections</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Move transactions between categories.</li>
            <li>Record changes in an audit trail.</li>
            <li>Recalculate affected months forward.</li>
            <li>Preserve the original bank record.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="deferred">
      <span class="section-number">05 / Spread spending</span>
      <h2>Large charges can affect several future budget periods</h2>
      <p class="lead">The user may spread a transaction such as a six-month auto-insurance payment across a chosen number of months. The first installment counts in the purchase month.</p>
      <div class="formula"><strong>Example</strong><br>$600 insurance charge ÷ 6 months = $100 budget impact in the purchase month + $100 in each of the next 5 months</div>
      <p>The user may later change the duration, causing remaining installments to be recalculated. The original transaction remains a single `$600` credit-card charge.</p>
      <div class="callout risk">
        <h3>Required accounting distinction</h3>
        <p>The selected behavior deducts only `$100` from the envelope each month even though the credit-card balance increased by `$600` immediately. This intentionally smooths budget impact, but it can make the envelope appear to contain money that is already owed to the card issuer. The product must always show both values: <strong>actual charge: $600</strong> and <strong>current-month budget impact: $100</strong>. It must never rewrite the bank transaction as six separate purchases.</p>
      </div>
      <p>This feature should be named “Spread budget impact” or similar, not “payment plan,” because it does not change what is owed to the credit-card company.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="transactions">
      <span class="section-number">06 / Transaction records</span>
      <h2>Clean at a glance, complete on demand</h2>
      <p>The transaction list should prioritize scanning. Each row displays only:</p>
      <div class="tags">
        <span class="tag">Date</span><span class="tag">Amount</span><span class="tag">Clean merchant name</span>
      </div>
      <p>Opening a transaction reveals its raw bank description, account or card nickname, pending or posted status, notes, original amount, category, spread schedule when applicable, and edit history.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Behavior</th><th>Safeguard</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Raw ingestion</strong></td><td>Preserve the exact provider description and identifiers.</td><td>Never store full card numbers or banking credentials.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Known mapping</strong></td><td>Reuse a user-confirmed merchant name when the same normalized bank descriptor returns.</td><td>User corrections remain editable.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>AI cleanup</strong></td><td>Suggest concise names such as “Chipotle,” “Whole Foods,” or “Amazon.”</td><td>Preserve raw text and clearly allow correction.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Display</strong></td><td>Use the cleaned name in lists and SMS messages.</td><td>Do not let AI alter amount, date, account, or transaction identity.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>The AI suggestion is presentation metadata, not the financial source of truth. Transaction amount and identity always come from the bank-data provider.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="sms">
      <span class="section-number">07 / Interactive notifications</span>
      <h2>Suggest first, then ask for confirmation</h2>
      <div class="conversation" aria-label="Example SMS conversation">
        <div class="bubble">New charge · A7
Chipotle · $18.42
Suggested: Dining

Reply Y to confirm or M for categories.</div>
        <div class="bubble user">Y</div>
        <div class="bubble">Added $18.42 to Dining.
Available: $85.33
You are at 72% of this envelope.</div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top: 28px;">Charges are handled one conversation at a time. Additional incoming transactions wait in a queue until the active charge is categorized or deferred. Unanswered charges remain uncategorized and visible in the web inbox.</p>
      <p>Supported interaction goals include:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Confirm the suggested category.</li>
        <li>Request a numbered category menu.</li>
        <li>Ask for a category balance.</li>
        <li>Ask for a monthly summary.</li>
        <li>Add a manual charge.</li>
        <li>Confirm or reject likely duplicates.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Near-limit and over-limit warnings should be included in categorization replies. Proactive warning delivery remains a configurable implementation decision.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="mvp">
      <span class="section-number">08 / Prototype and MVP</span>
      <h2>Validate the behavior before paying for integrations</h2>
      <p class="lead">The first prototype remains a web application with simulated bank synchronization and a simulated SMS conversation. It validates the product model while preserving clean boundaries for real providers later.</p>
      <div class="grid-2">
        <article class="panel">
          <h3>Prototype includes</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Guided account setup.</li>
            <li>Category templates.</li>
            <li>50/30/20 or custom allocation.</li>
            <li>Editable envelope targets.</li>
            <li>Simulated incoming bank charges.</li>
            <li>AI-like merchant cleanup with corrections.</li>
            <li>Interactive SMS simulator.</li>
            <li>Category summary and transaction details.</li>
            <li>Uncategorized inbox.</li>
            <li>Manual month close and rollover.</li>
            <li>Spread budget impact.</li>
            <li>Audit history and warnings.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
        <article class="panel">
          <h3>Deferred from prototype</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Live bank aggregator.</li>
            <li>Live SMS provider and phone number.</li>
            <li>CSV card exports.</li>
            <li>Spreadsheet migration.</li>
            <li>Refund reconciliation.</li>
            <li>Split transactions.</li>
            <li>Checking and savings accounts.</li>
            <li>Shared household budgets.</li>
            <li>Native mobile applications.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p>The production architecture should isolate bank connectivity, SMS delivery, merchant normalization, and budgeting rules behind separate interfaces. This allows simulated providers to be replaced without rewriting the budgeting product.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="decisions">
      <span class="section-number">09 / Production decisions</span>
      <h2>Questions intentionally left open</h2>
      <p>These do not block a prototype, but they must be resolved before real financial data or SMS is enabled.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Bank-data provider</strong></td><td>Coverage, refresh timing, pending transactions, pricing, OAuth behavior, and production approval vary among providers.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>SMS provider</strong></td><td>United States registration, consent, opt-out handling, sender type, throughput, and cost must be evaluated.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>AI merchant service</strong></td><td>Determine whether transaction descriptions may be sent to an external model, how data is retained, and whether rules-first cleanup is sufficient.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Pending reconciliation</strong></td><td>A pending charge may disappear, post for a different amount, or receive a different merchant description.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Refund behavior</strong></td><td>Define whether a refund reverses the original category and any remaining spread schedule.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Month closure</strong></td><td>Decide whether uncategorized charges block closing and whether closed months can be reopened.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Credential recovery</strong></td><td>Username/password authentication needs secure hashing, reset, lockout, session expiry, and account recovery.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Hosting and retention</strong></td><td>Choose managed hosting, encrypted storage, secrets management, backups, data export, and deletion policy.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="callout info">
        <h3>Connection failure behavior already decided</h3>
        <p>If bank synchronization stops, notify the user by text, show a persistent dashboard warning, support reconnection without duplicates, and permit manual entry during the gap.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="acceptance">
      <span class="section-number">10 / Prototype acceptance criteria</span>
      <h2>Evidence required before calling the prototype successful</h2>
      <ol class="acceptance">
        <li>A new user can choose a starter template and a 50/30/20 or custom allocation without beginning from an empty category screen.</li>
        <li>The setup calculates suggested category amounts from the monthly budgeting total and allows every amount to be changed.</li>
        <li>A simulated connected card can produce a new transaction containing amount, date, account nickname, and raw description.</li>
        <li>The product displays an editable cleaned merchant name while preserving the original bank description in transaction detail.</li>
        <li>The simulated SMS experience suggests a category and accepts confirmation or a numbered alternate selection.</li>
        <li>Unanswered transactions remain in an uncategorized inbox and do not disappear.</li>
        <li>Categorizing a transaction updates the envelope and displays near-limit or over-limit status.</li>
        <li>Month closure adds each active category's contribution and carries positive and negative balances forward.</li>
        <li>Historical transaction corrections recalculate affected envelope balances through later months and retain an audit record.</li>
        <li>A `$600` transaction spread across six months remains one `$600` bank charge while showing `$100` of budget impact in the purchase month and five later months.</li>
        <li>Changing a spread duration recalculates its remaining monthly impacts without modifying the original bank transaction.</li>
        <li>The interface distinguishes actual card charges from smoothed budget impact wherever the two totals differ.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <div class="closing">
      <h2>Approved direction: connected data, guided setup, deliberate categorization.</h2>
      <p>This definition supersedes the earlier forwarded-bank-text workflow. No implementation should begin by assuming the original SMS source. The prototype should model bank synchronization and interactive app-originated texts, while real providers remain replaceable integration decisions.</p>
    </div>
  </main>

  <footer>
    <span>Connected Envelope Budget · Product definition</span>
    <span>Updated September 11, 2026 · Pre-prototype baseline</span>
  </footer>
</body>
</html>
