+<body>
+  <header class="hero">
+    <div class="hero-inner">
+      <div>
+        <p class="eyebrow">A product manager's AI-assisted build journey</p>
+        <h1>From product idea to working prototype</h1>
+        <p class="hero-dek">How curiosity about higher-order prompt scaffolding became an approved product plan, a professional web stack, and the first working slice of an AI-era budgeting application.</p>
+      </div>
+      <aside class="hero-note">
+        <strong>Journey status</strong>
+        <p>Product direction approved and the first full-stack vertical slice validated on September 11, 2026.</p>
+      </aside>
+    </div>
+  </header>
+
+  <nav aria-label="Journey sections">
+    <div class="nav-inner">
+      <a href="#abstract">Abstract</a>
+      <a href="#journey">Journey map</a>
+      <a href="#shift">The shift</a>
+      <a href="#artifacts">Artifacts</a>
+      <a href="#learning">What I learned</a>
+      <a href="#proof">What exists now</a>
+      <a href="#next">Next chapter</a>
+    </div>
+  </nav>
+
+  <main>
+    <section id="abstract">
+      <div class="abstract-grid">
+        <div>
+          <p class="eyebrow"><span class="section-number">01</span> Summary / abstract</p>
+          <h2>A budgeting app built for the AI world we now live in</h2>
+          <p class="section-intro">The goal was to rethink budgeting around something people increasingly understand: communicating with an AI agent. Instead of making users navigate a rigid ledger, the product uses familiar, conversational interactions to help categorize spending, maintain envelopes, and make routine decisions on the user's behalf.</p>
+          <p>The ambition was not to automate the user out of budgeting. It was to automate the repetitive work <em>without separating the user from each individual purchase</em>. Every transaction still creates a brief moment of recognition: what was bought, how much it cost, and where it belongs.</p>
+          <p>That interaction matters because awareness is not only about the total amount spent. It is also about the sheer number of purchases being made. A short confirmation for each charge keeps the user grounded in both dimensions while AI handles merchant cleanup, recommendations, and the mechanics of updating the budget.</p>
+        </div>
+        <aside class="principle">
+          <span class="artifact-label">Defining product principle</span>
+          <blockquote>Automate the work of budgeting, not the awareness that makes budgeting valuable.</blockquote>
+        </aside>
+      </div>
+    </section>
+
+    <section id="journey">
+      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="section-number">02</span> Journey map</p>
+      <h2>Six phases from curiosity to evidence</h2>
+      <p class="section-intro">The work did not begin with code. Each phase produced an artifact or decision that made the next phase more grounded, reducing the chance of building a polished version of an unclear idea.</p>
+
+      <div class="journey-map" aria-label="Six-phase project journey">
+        <article class="phase" data-step="01">
+          <span class="phase-kicker">Orientation</span>
+          <h3>Understand the stack</h3>
+          <p>Started as a product manager new to technical stacks, unpacking how a frontend, API, database, tooling, and local environment work together.</p>
+          <span class="phase-outcome">Outcome: a usable mental model</span>
+        </article>
+        <article class="phase" data-step="02">
+          <span class="phase-kicker">Prompt design</span>
+          <h3>Build the interview</h3>
+          <p>Moved from one-shot prompting to a structured, multi-round discovery process that required the AI to ask clarifying questions before proposing a solution.</p>
+          <span class="phase-outcome">Outcome: prompt architecture</span>
+        </article>
+        <article class="phase" data-step="03">
+          <span class="phase-kicker">Product discovery</span>
+          <h3>Pressure-test the idea</h3>
+          <p>Iterated through bank connectivity, app-originated SMS, envelopes, rollover, refunds, security, merchant cleanup, and AI-assisted review.</p>
+          <span class="phase-outcome">Outcome: explicit decisions</span>
+        </article>
+        <article class="phase" data-step="04">
+          <span class="phase-kicker">Definition</span>
+          <h3>Approve the direction</h3>
+          <p>Consolidated the product into a source-of-truth plan with workflows, constraints, MVP boundaries, operating posture, and acceptance criteria.</p>
+          <span class="phase-outcome">Outcome: approved product plan</span>
+        </article>
+        <article class="phase" data-step="05">
+          <span class="phase-kicker">Foundation</span>
+          <h3>Prepare the machine</h3>
+          <p>Audited and installed the development stack, established the frontend and backend projects, configured package sources, and connected the layers.</p>
+          <span class="phase-outcome">Outcome: working environment</span>
+        </article>
+        <article class="phase" data-step="06">
+          <span class="phase-kicker">Vertical slice</span>
+          <h3>Prove the concept</h3>
+          <p>Built guided onboarding with templates, 50/30/20 targets, editable envelopes, an API, and SQLite persistence, then tested save and reload.</p>
+          <span class="phase-outcome">Outcome: validated prototype</span>
+        </article>
+      </div>
+    </section>
+
+    <section id="shift" class="turning-point">
+      <div class="question-stack" aria-label="Questions that improved the product definition">
+        <div class="question">What if the app sent the notification?</div>
+        <div class="question">How involved should the user remain?</div>
+        <div class="question">What must persist behind the screen?</div>
+      </div>
+      <div>
+        <p class="eyebrow"><span class="section-number">03</span> The important shift</p>
+        <h2>From asking AI for answers to asking AI for better questions</h2>
+        <p class="section-intro">The quality of the product improved when the AI stopped behaving like a rapid solution generator and began behaving like a discovery partner. A two-to-three-round interview created space to challenge assumptions, expose dependencies, and turn instinctive “what if” ideas into product decisions.</p>
+        <p>The prompt became part of the product process. It established roles, phases, decision gates, technical constraints, verification expectations, and clear instructions to pause for clarification. That scaffolding made the AI's broader knowledge available without surrendering product judgment.</p>
+      </div>
+    </section>
+
+    <section id="artifacts">
+      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="section-number">04</span> Artifact inventory</p>
+      <h2>The work became tangible in four forms</h2>
+      <p class="section-intro">Each artifact answered a different question: how to work, what to build, what it runs on, and whether the experience actually works.</p>
+
+      <div class="artifact-grid">
+        <article class="artifact">
+          <span class="artifact-label">01 / Prompt architecture</span>
+          <h3>The operating instructions</h3>
+          <p>A phased kickoff scaffold taught the agent how to audit the environment, interview the product owner, wait at decision gates, create a vertical slice, and verify its own work.</p>
+          <a href="Kickoff_Prompt_New_Project_Scaffold.md">View the kickoff scaffold</a>
+        </article>
+        <article class="artifact">
+          <span class="artifact-label">02 / Product plan</span>
+          <h3>The approved direction</h3>
+          <p>A comprehensive definition captured the product intent, transaction workflow, onboarding, envelope behavior, AI review, refunds, SMS, account controls, MVP, and acceptance criteria.</p>
+          <a href="connected_budget_product_definition.html">View the product definition</a>
+        </article>
+        <article class="artifact">
+          <span class="artifact-label">03 / Tech stack</span>
+          <h3>The delivery foundation</h3>
+          <p>A professional local environment joined a React and TypeScript interface to an ASP.NET Core API and SQLite database, with build, formatting, package, and browser-testing tools around it.</p>
+          <div class="stack-strip" aria-label="Technology stack">
+            <div class="stack-item"><strong>React</strong><span>Interface</span></div>
+            <div class="stack-item"><strong>.NET</strong><span>API</span></div>
+            <div class="stack-item"><strong>EF Core</strong><span>Data access</span></div>
+            <div class="stack-item"><strong>SQLite</strong><span>Persistence</span></div>
+          </div>
+        </article>
+        <article class="artifact">
+          <span class="artifact-label">04 / Prototype</span>
+          <h3>The first working evidence</h3>
+          <p>A three-step onboarding flow lets a user choose a budget template, set monthly take-home income, review suggested 50/30/20 envelopes, edit targets, and save the plan through the API.</p>
+          <a href="connected-envelope-budget/frontend/">Open the prototype source</a>
+        </article>
+      </div>
+    </section>
+
+    <section id="learning">
+      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="section-number">05</span> What I learned</p>
+      <h2>The lessons extended beyond one budgeting app</h2>
+      <p class="section-intro">This project changed how I think about prototypes, prompting, and the relationship between product vision and technical foundations.</p>
+
+      <ol class="learning-list">
+        <li>
+          <div>
+            <h3>A professional prototype is an ecosystem, not a single application</h3>
+            <p>I learned that numerous applications and tools are necessary to create more professionally constructed prototypes for web-based products. The interface is only one layer. Runtimes, package managers, an API, a database, source control, formatting, compilation, and browser validation all contribute to whether a prototype is credible and repeatable.</p>
+          </div>
+        </li>
+        <li>
+          <div>
+            <h3>Product vision improves when AI is required to clarify</h3>
+            <p>I learned the importance of iterating on a product vision with AI and explicitly requiring it to ask clarifying questions rather than jump straight to a conclusion. A simplistic prototype can move only as fast as my own “what if it did this?” creativity. A structured dialogue uses AI's much broader ability to ask “what if?” and “how do you envision this working?” while leaving the final decisions with me.</p>
+          </div>
+        </li>
+        <li>
+          <div>
+            <h3>Backend thinking turns a story into a system</h3>
+            <p>I learned the importance of thinking through how information will be stored, related, changed, and retrieved. Frontend work is powerful for storytelling, but a well-considered backend and database allow the prototype to retain state, support richer behavior, integrate with larger systems, and evolve into something that can be demonstrated repeatedly and scaled responsibly.</p>
+          </div>
+        </li>
+        <li>
+          <div>
+            <h3>Verification is part of prototyping</h3>
+            <p>I learned that “it looks right” is not the same as “it works.” Building the complete path from screen to API to database, then testing save, reload, CORS, and responsive layouts, created evidence that the concept was more than a visual mockup.</p>
+          </div>
+        </li>
+      </ol>
+    </section>
+
+    <section id="proof">
+      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="section-number">06</span> What exists now</p>
+      <h2>A small slice, proven all the way through</h2>
+      <p class="section-intro">The current prototype intentionally stops before bank integrations, transaction processing, SMS, and the AI review assistant. Its purpose is to prove the foundation on which those experiences can be built.</p>
+      <div class="proof-band">
+        <div class="metric"><strong>3 steps</strong><span class="metric-label">Guided onboarding</span></div>
+        <div class="metric"><strong>3 templates</strong><span class="metric-label">Starting structures</span></div>
+        <div class="metric"><strong>50 / 30 / 20</strong><span class="metric-label">Initial allocation</span></div>
+        <div class="metric"><strong>200 OK</strong><span class="metric-label">Persisted API reload</span></div>
+      </div>
+    </section>
+
+    <section class="reflection">
+      <p class="eyebrow">A changed perspective</p>
+      <blockquote>Before this journey, a prototype primarily meant a screen that could tell the story. Now it means a structured conversation, an explicit product model, a working system behind the interface, and evidence that the idea behaves as intended.</blockquote>
+    </section>
+
+    <section id="next" class="next-step">
+      <div>
+        <p class="eyebrow"><span class="section-number">07</span> The next chapter</p>
+        <h2>Transition from building to feedback</h2>
+        <p>The next step is not to add every planned feature. It is to put the current onboarding experience in front of users, observe where they hesitate, test whether the envelope model is understandable, and use that evidence to refine the product before implementing connected transactions, interactive SMS, and AI categorization.</p>
+        <a class="button" href="connected_budget_product_definition.html">Read the approved product plan</a>
+      </div>
+      <aside class="stamp">
+        <strong>Current milestone</strong>
+        Product direction approved<br>
+        First vertical slice built<br>
+        Persistence verified<br>
+        Ready for feedback
+      </aside>
+    </section>
+  </main>
+
+  <footer>
+    <span>Connected Envelope Budget journey</span>
+    <span>Documented September 11, 2026</span>
+  </footer>
+</body>
+</html>
